    "grade": "10-12",
    "points": 3,
    "timeMinutes": null,
    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "If $n$ is a natural number then we define $n!$ to be the product $n\\times(n-1)\\times(n-2)\\times\\cdots\\times2\\times1$. For example $4!=4\\times3\\times2\\times1=24$. If $6!=a!\\times b!$ where $a>1$ and $b>1$, then $a+b$ is",
    "image": null,
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "8"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "7"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "6"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "5"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "4"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "A",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Use the prime 5",
        "body": "$6!=720=6\\cdot5\\cdot4\\cdot3\\cdot2\\cdot1$ is divisible by 5, so one of $a,b$ is at least 5; and neither exceeds 5 (since $6!/6!=1$ would force the other to be 1). So one factor is $5!=120$ and the other is $720/120=6=3!$. Hence $\\{a,b\\}=\\{3,5\\}$ and $a+b=8$.\n\n**Answer: (A) 8**"
      }
    ],
    "topics": [
      "number-theory"
    ],
    "difficulty": 2,
    "duplicateOf": null,
    "reviewNote": null
  },
